# 1. COMPANY AND BRAND CONTEXT

## Brand name

CARO

Always write the brand as CARO or Caro, depending on the visual context.

The name is short, memorable, modern and approachable.

Do not use the previous company name or invent alternative brand names.

## What Caro does

Caro is a platform that makes vehicle ownership more accessible by allowing people to discover, form and participate in collective vehicle ownership opportunities.

Traditionally, purchasing a vehicle means one person must independently finance and own the entire vehicle.

Caro introduces another possibility.

People can come together to acquire vehicles, contribute according to their agreed ownership interests, share ownership responsibilities and coordinate vehicle access.

A person can start with a small ownership interest in one vehicle and gradually build interests across multiple vehicles.

For example:
- Someone can participate in owning a practical daily driver.
- They can also acquire an interest in a performance car.
- They might join another group to participate in owning an SUV.
- Over time, they can build a diverse personal vehicle portfolio.

Caro is not simply a car-sharing application, a car rental company or a luxury car club.

It is building an ecosystem around collective vehicle acquisition, ownership management, access coordination and a network of dealerships and partners.

## The bigger vision

Caro wants to transform the way people think about vehicle ownership.

Instead of asking:

"Which car can I afford to buy entirely?"

Caro introduces a different question:

"What vehicles could I own a share of, with the budget I have?"

The long-term ambition is to create a platform where people can build their own digital garages containing ownership interests in multiple vehicles.

Caro will connect consumers, dealerships, vehicle owners, financing providers, insurance providers and other relevant partners.

The initial focus is cars. Future expansion into other high-value assets should not dominate the landing page.

